raw='The history of secret writing is also a history of people finding patterns. A sender may replace every letter with another symbol and hope that the message will remain hidden. Yet the habits of a language survive in surprising ways. Common letters still occur often, repeated words leave repeated shapes, and familiar phrases provide clues. A careful reader does not treat any single clue as proof. Instead the reader makes a guess, checks its consequences, and changes the guess when the evidence disagrees. Computers make this process faster but do not remove the need for a clear model of the problem. When a short key is repeated across a long message, the same positions in the key return again and again. Splitting the message into columns can reveal a simple pattern inside each column. Enough text gives the analyst a better chance of distinguishing a useful pattern from a coincidence. The lesson is that a large number of possible keys is only one part of security. We must also ask what information remains visible and what an observer can learn from it. Good engineering starts by stating the assumptions, checking examples, and looking for ways that a system can fail. A secure design must survive careful public study rather than depend on keeping the method unknown.'
assert norm(raw)==CORPUS
cut=raw.index('When a short key')
S('A5 / Recovered Vigenère prose, part 1',[P(raw[:cut])],section='APPENDIX',take='Word boundaries and punctuation are editorial reconstructions, not encoded by the cipher.')
S('A6 / Recovered Vigenère prose, part 2',[P(raw[cut:])],section='APPENDIX')

S('A7 / Deriving the finite-sample IC formula',[('p', 'There are N(N − 1) ordered pairs of distinct positions.'), ('p', 'For a letter a, exactly fₐ(fₐ − 1) pairs have a in both positions. These events are disjoint across letters.'), ('math', '\\operatorname{IC}(X)=\\frac{\\sum_{a=0}^{25}f_a(f_a-1)}{N(N-1)}'), ('math', '=\\sum_{a=0}^{25}\\frac{f_a}{N}\\frac{f_a-1}{N-1}.')],section='APPENDIX',take='',source='')

S('A8 / A small IC calculation, fully worked',[('code', 'STRING', 'AABAAB', 19), ('table', ['Letter', 'Count fₐ', 'fₐ(fₐ − 1)'], [['A', '4', '12'], ['B', '2', '2'], ['All others', '0', '0']], [260, 260, 336]), ('math', '\\operatorname{IC}(\\text{AABAAB})=\\frac{12+2}{6\\cdot5}=\\frac7{15}\\approx0.4667.')],section='APPENDIX',take='Count matching pairs; do not divide by N² when positions must be distinct.',source='')

S('A9 / From sample counts to language probabilities',[('p', 'If independent letters have probabilities q₀, …, q₂₅, then the probability of equality is:'), ('math', '\\Pr[X_i=X_j]=\\sum_{a=0}^{25}q_a^2\\qquad(i\\ne j).'), ('p', 'Writing q̂ₐ = fₐ/N gives an exact relation for the observed sample:'), ('math', '\\operatorname{IC}(X)=\\frac{N\\sum_a\\widehat q_a^{\\,2}-1}{N-1}\\;\\approx\\;\\sum_a\\widehat q_a^{\\,2}.')],section='APPENDIX',take='The approximation improves with N. Real language also has dependencies between positions.',source='')

S('A10 / Score a candidate using all letter counts',[('p', 'Let Oₐ(k) count plaintext letter a after subtracting candidate shift k from the column.'), ('math', 'E_a=Nq_a,\\qquad\\chi^2(k)=\\sum_{a=0}^{25}\\frac{(O_a(k)-E_a)^2}{E_a}.'), ('p', 'The qₐ values form an illustrative English frequency model, normalized to sum to 1.'), ('p', 'A smaller score means a closer fit to that model. This is a ranking heuristic, not proof that a key is correct.')],section='APPENDIX',take='The full model probabilities are included in Appendix A4.',source='')

